WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.720
Dwarf galaxies hold on to only 15% of the oxygen their stars create. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 248. Models of a lifeless rocky atmosphere, colliding spiral galaxies, and the timing of techno-signature searches follow. Hubble, according to Universe Today, measured both the oxygen 37 nearby dwarf galaxies still hold, and the oxygen they have lost across their lifetimes. What remains sits mostly in gas, with smaller shares locked in stars and

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:35.120
and dust, while radiation from massive stars and core-collapse supernovae have driven

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.800
the rest into the circumgalactic medium and beyond.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:43.940
The Glow project folds new and archival data from Hubble, Spitzer, the very large array,

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:49.460
and the Australian telescope Compact Array to follow gas and heavy elements among low-mass

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:52.780
galaxies, their halos and the intergalactic medium.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:58.340
Grace Telford, second author on the paper, and an assistant professor of physics and astronomy

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:03.740
at the University of Utah, noted that the light and winds of heavy stars and the supernovae

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:08.520
that finished them push oxygen-rich gas out of these galaxies as time goes on.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:14.880
Most of that oxygen is forged in core-collapse supernovae, the explosive deaths of massive stars,

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:20.240
though a very small fraction also comes from other supernovae when remnants of low mass stars

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:25.780
become unstable or merge. Glow is a large-scale effort jointly funded by the National

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:30.680
aeronautics and space administration, or NASA, and the National Science Foundation.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:36.020
And massive galaxies like the Milky Way have had lost oxygen, tallied in similar ways.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:41.060
Computer models of galaxy growth still fall short of the lost scene in these dwarfs.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:44.460
A gap tied to gravity two week to keep the gas at home.

00:01:45.444 --> 00:01:50.644
A dwarf galaxy may span only a few thousand light years from edge to edge, and still form

00:01:50.644 --> 00:01:51.884
stars at a steady rate.

00:01:51.884 --> 00:01:56.504
That is a small island of stars, and the whole system is light enough that the energy

00:01:56.504 --> 00:02:00.044
from a handful of supernovae can blow material clean out.

00:02:00.504 --> 00:02:02.804
Set that island beside a familiar yardstick.

00:02:03.344 --> 00:02:07.584
The continental United States runs roughly 3,000 miles from coast to coast.

00:02:08.024 --> 00:02:12.984
Adorf galaxy is thousands of times larger in linear extent than that coast to coast run.

00:02:13.524 --> 00:02:18.444
Yet its gravitational grip stays fragile enough that a few energetic events can unbind its

00:02:18.444 --> 00:02:18.664
gas.

00:02:18.664 --> 00:02:24.324
A few thousand light years is modest next to a giant spiral, and that modest size is why

00:02:24.324 --> 00:02:27.284
a handful of explosions can empty so much of the reservoir.

00:02:27.784 --> 00:02:32.544
In the Milky Way you get about one oxygen atom mixed into 2,000 atoms of hydrogen.

00:02:33.204 --> 00:02:38.644
In these dwarfs that count falls to one oxygen among 10,000 to 20,000 hydrogen atoms.

00:02:39.364 --> 00:02:44.284
The drop comes because the weaker pull cannot hold the heated metal-loaded gas that starlight

00:02:44.284 --> 00:02:46.944
pressure and supernova shock waves throw outward.

00:02:47.504 --> 00:02:52.484
Bigger galaxies lose metal-rich gas, too, but dwarfs sit in shallower gravitational

00:02:52.484 --> 00:02:57.444
wells, so the gas can leave more easily and more thoroughly than it can from a heavy

00:02:57.444 --> 00:02:57.964
spiral.

00:02:58.624 --> 00:03:02.784
Massive stars couple their energy to the surrounding gas through more than one route.

00:03:03.384 --> 00:03:04.624
Radiation is one channel.

00:03:05.244 --> 00:03:06.364
Cosmic rays are another.

00:03:07.184 --> 00:03:09.384
Supernovae going off in clusters are a third.

00:03:10.024 --> 00:03:15.024
Which of those channels does the most efficient job of driving gas out is not settled, and it

00:03:15.024 --> 00:03:19.924
is not settled whether a bursty history of star formation, or a quieter one, changes

00:03:19.924 --> 00:03:21.384
how well that driving works.

00:03:21.984 --> 00:03:26.924
The exact mix of forces that sets how much gas leaves any one dwarf is still not known.

00:03:28.154 --> 00:03:34.094
Astronomers, Space.com reports, ran atmospheric models of a rocky planet with no life to learn

00:03:34.094 --> 00:03:37.054
how its chemistry would evolve without biological activity.

00:03:37.634 --> 00:03:42.134
Those runs yield spectral fingerprints that do not match the patterns expected when life

00:03:42.134 --> 00:03:42.734
is present.

00:03:42.734 --> 00:03:48.134
Giving observers clearer criteria for reading future telescope data on exoplanet air.

00:03:48.714 --> 00:03:54.014
The models watch gases that living chemistry would change or create, so a world whose atmosphere

00:03:54.014 --> 00:03:56.534
looks too inert for biology can be flagged.

00:03:56.894 --> 00:04:01.574
How different host stars might alter that baseline lifeless case remains an open question.

00:04:02.174 --> 00:04:07.554
Some false positives could still mimic biological signals even in that lifeless baseline, and

00:04:07.554 --> 00:04:08.874
that puzzle is not closed.

00:04:08.874 --> 00:04:13.879
Gemini North hosted high school students in the project Hokulani Internship,

00:04:14.399 --> 00:04:18.959
Astronomy Magazine reports, who selected a pair of clashing spirals as the target.

00:04:19.419 --> 00:04:27.259
The pair is catalogued as NGC 7253, also known as ARP 278. The 8.1 meter telescope

00:04:27.259 --> 00:04:32.319
recorded the image in October 2025. That frame is the highest resolution view

00:04:32.319 --> 00:04:38.188
obtained of these galaxies. Project Phoenix, Fizz.org reports, targeted roughly

00:04:38.188 --> 00:04:45.308
1000 sun-like stars for techno signatures between 2012 and 2015, under the search for extra-terrestrial

00:04:45.308 --> 00:04:50.788
intelligence, or SETI, a re-analysis using game theory argues that when observers listen,

00:04:51.368 --> 00:04:56.148
could prove more decisive than which stars they choose, and that optimal listening windows could

00:04:56.148 --> 00:05:01.861
matter more than the star list itself. Primordial black holes would have formed directly from dense

00:05:01.861 --> 00:05:07.201
clumps in the early universe. Fresh analysis excludes the largest hypothesized examples

00:05:07.201 --> 00:05:09.601
from contributing significantly to dark matter.

